\subsection{Dual Mechanism: Normalization Interference and Gradient Flow Absence}
\label{sec:why_gradient}

\textbf{Convergent evidence.} Four independent lines of evidence---gradient norms, CKA representational similarity, normalization ablation, and downstream behavioral metrics---converge on a dual-mechanism account of the Detach degradation. The Detach+LN ablation (Section~\ref{sec:why_detach}) decomposes the 3.4\,pp Detach deficit into two roughly comparable factors: BatchNorm interference ($\sim$56\%, 1.9\,pp recovered by replacing BN with LN) and gradient flow absence ($\sim$44\%, 1.5\,pp residual gap). The Detach+LN 95\% CI [.726, .804] overlaps with both Detach and Vanilla, precluding assignment of a dominant factor.

Gradient norms and CKA provide mechanistic detail for the gradient-flow component: encoder gradient norms fall to 1--13\% of Vanilla under Detach, and CKA with pretrained features drops to 0.31--0.54 (vs.\ $\geq$0.95 for Vanilla, Random OCC, and Full OCC). These measurements form a causal chain: gradient collapse $\to$ representational drift $\to$ behavioral degradation. Random OCC---trained with random binary labels---preserves pretrained representations (CKA 0.968/0.956) as well as Vanilla and Full OCC, confirming across all measurement types that gradient flow presence, not label content, is the operative factor for encoder preservation.

