\subsection{Decomposing the Detach Degradation}
\label{sec:why_detach}

Detach OCC falls 3.4\,pp below Vanilla despite identical architecture and forward computation to Full OCC. Two mechanisms contribute: (1)~the auxiliary head's BatchNorm2d layers update running statistics at every forward pass regardless of gradient routing, perturbing the encoder's internal statistics; (2)~\texttt{stop\_gradient} blocks auxiliary gradients from reaching the encoder, causing gradient collapse (1--13\% of Vanilla norms).

Replacing BatchNorm2d with LayerNorm---which normalizes per-sample without cross-batch running statistics---while keeping \texttt{stop\_gradient} (Detach+LN) recovers 1.9\,pp of the 3.4\,pp deficit (CAR 0.765 $\pm$ 0.023 vs.\ Detach 0.746 $\pm$ 0.002). The remaining 1.5\,pp gap to Vanilla (0.780) is attributable to gradient flow absence alone. Since the 95\% CI for Detach+LN [.726, .804] overlaps with both endpoints, we characterize the two contributions as roughly comparable rather than assigning dominance to either factor. This dual-mechanism account yields two complementary practical recommendations: use per-sample normalization (LayerNorm) in auxiliary heads to avoid cross-batch interference, and maintain gradient flow to prevent encoder gradient collapse.

